## Memory Management in C++

C++ gives programmers *direct* control over how and where memory lives.  
At the same time the language provides a set of idioms, abstractions, and
standard tools that help you write safe, efficient, and maintainable code.

Below is a practical, layered view that walks through the most common
mechanisms:

| Layer | What it is | Typical use | Gotchas |
|-------|------------|-------------|---------|
| **Stack** | Automatic storage duration | Local variables, function parameters, recursion | Size limited by OS stack; overflow → crash |
| **Heap** | Dynamic storage duration | Objects you need to outlive a function, variable‑size data | Manual `new`/`delete`; leaks, dangling pointers, fragmentation |
| **RAII / Smart pointers** | Automatic cleanup of heap objects | Most C++ code that uses dynamic memory | Be careful with shared ownership & circular refs |
| **Containers & Allocators** | Standardised, exception‑safe containers that manage memory internally | `std::vector`, `std::map`, etc. | Understand reallocation, iterator invalidation |
| **Custom allocators & pools** | Specialized memory sources for performance | Games, network servers | Complex, error‑prone if not needed |
| **Debugging & instrumentation** | Tools to detect misuse | Valgrind, ASAN, LeakSanitizer | Requires extra build steps, runtime overhead |

Let’s dive into each layer.

---

## 1. Stack (Automatic Storage Duration)

```cpp
void foo() {
    int a = 10;            // stored on the stack
    double arr[20];        // contiguous block on the stack
}
```

- **Lifetime**: Begins when the variable is defined, ends when its block ends (i.e., when the function returns or a block is exited).
- **Speed**: Allocation/deallocation are just pointer arithmetic – *O(1)*.
- **Size limit**: OS‑defined (often a few megabytes). Stack overflow occurs if you create too many local objects or recurse too deep.
- **Safety**: No need to free; compiler guarantees destruction (for objects with destructors).

**Pitfall**: Returning a pointer/reference to a stack object is undefined behaviour.

```cpp
int* bad() { int x = 42; return &x; }   // BAD
```

---

## 2. Heap (Dynamic Storage Duration)

The heap is a contiguous region of memory that the runtime allocates on demand. You control it via `new`/`delete` (or `new[]`/`delete[]`).

```cpp
int* p = new int(5);      // allocation
delete p;                 // deallocation

int* arr = new int[10];   // array allocation
delete[] arr;             // array deallocation
```

### Operator `new` / `delete`

C++ standard defines `operator new` (allocation) and `operator delete` (deallocation). They can be overloaded per class or globally.

```cpp
struct My {
    void* operator new(std::size_t size) {
        std::cout << "Allocating My of size " << size << '\n';
        return ::operator new(size);      // call global new
    }
    void operator delete(void* ptr) {
        std::cout << "Freeing My\n";
        ::operator delete(ptr);           // call global delete
    }
};
```

**Placement new**: construct an object in pre‑allocated storage.

```cpp
char buffer[sizeof(My)];
My* p = new (buffer) My;   // placement new
p->~My();                  // manual destructor call
```

### Common Errors

| Error | Why it happens | Symptoms |
|-------|----------------|----------|
| **Memory leak** | Forgetting `delete` or `delete[]`. | Memory usage grows; tools flag it. |
| **Dangling pointer** | Using a pointer after deletion or after object lifetime ends. | Segfault, corrupt data. |
| **Double delete** | Deleting the same pointer twice. | Undefined behaviour, crash. |
| **Mismatched new/delete** | Using `new[]` with `delete`, or vice versa. | Wrong destructor calls, heap corruption. |
| **Buffer overflow** | Writing beyond allocated bounds. | Corrupted heap metadata → crash. |

---

## 3. RAII (Resource Acquisition Is Initialization)

RAII couples the lifetime of a resource (memory, file handles, sockets) to the lifetime of an object. When the object goes out of scope, its destructor frees the resource.

```cpp
struct Buffer {
    char* data;
    std::size_t size;
    Buffer(std::size_t sz) : size(sz) {
        data = new char[size];
    }
    ~Buffer() { delete[] data; }
};
```

Because the destructor runs automatically, you avoid leaks even in the presence of exceptions.

**Rule of Five**: If you manage resources manually, you typically need to implement (or delete) copy/move constructors/assignment operators.

```cpp
Buffer(const Buffer&) = delete;          // prevent copying
Buffer& operator=(const Buffer&) = delete;
Buffer(Buffer&& other) noexcept : data(other.data), size(other.size) {
    other.data = nullptr; other.size = 0;
}
Buffer& operator=(Buffer&& other) noexcept {
    if (this != &other) { 
        delete[] data; 
        data = other.data; size = other.size;
        other.data = nullptr; other.size = 0;
    }
    return *this;
}
```

---

## 4. Smart Pointers

Smart pointers are RAII wrappers around raw pointers that manage object lifetime automatically.

| Pointer type | Ownership semantics | Typical use |
|--------------|--------------------|-------------|
| `std::unique_ptr<T>` | Unique ownership; non‑copyable, movable | Most single‑owner scenarios |
| `std::shared_ptr<T>` | Shared ownership; reference‑counted | Multiple owners, graphs |
| `std::weak_ptr<T>` | Non‑owning, observes `shared_ptr` | Break circular references |

### Example

```cpp
std::unique_ptr<int> p1 = std::make_unique<int>(42);
// *p1 is valid here
// No delete needed – destructor frees when p1 goes out of scope

std::shared_ptr<int> p2 = std::make_shared<int>(99);
std::shared_ptr<int> p3 = p2;  // shared
// Object destroyed when p2 and p3 go out of scope
```

**Common pitfalls**

- **Dangling `weak_ptr`**: `weak_ptr::lock()` returns `nullptr` if the object is already destroyed.
- **Circular references**: Two `shared_ptr`s pointing to each other can keep the objects alive forever. Use `weak_ptr` to break the cycle.

### Custom Deleters

```cpp
std::unique_ptr<FILE, decltype(&fclose)> file(
    fopen("log.txt", "w"), &fclose);
```

---

## 5. Standard Containers & Allocators

Containers (`std::vector`, `std::list`, `std::map`, etc.) manage memory internally. You usually don't touch raw pointers at all.

```cpp
std::vector<int> v = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
v.push_back(6);   // may reallocate internally
```

- **Reallocation**: `std::vector` may allocate a larger block and copy/move elements, invalidating pointers/references/iterators to the old data. Use `reserve()` to pre‑allocate capacity.

- **Allocator interface**: You can provide a custom allocator to a container to control how memory is obtained.

```cpp
template<typename T>
struct MyAllocator {
    using value_type = T;
    T* allocate(std::size_t n) {
        return static_cast<T*>(::operator new(n * sizeof(T)));
    }
    void deallocate(T* p, std::size_t) noexcept {
        ::operator delete(p);
    }
    // ... (other optional types and functions)
};

std::vector<int, MyAllocator<int>> vec;
```

> **Note**: Modern C++ containers are *exception‑safe*; they will clean up automatically if an exception is thrown during an operation.

---

## 6. Custom Allocators & Memory Pools

For performance‑critical code, you may want to avoid many small `operator new` calls and instead allocate large blocks (pools, arenas) and carve out sub‑blocks yourself.

- **Object pools**: Useful when objects are the same size (e.g., game entities). Pre‑allocate a large chunk and hand out pieces.

```cpp
class ObjectPool {
public:
    ObjectPool(std::size_t count) {
        pool = std::malloc(count * sizeof(MyObject));
    }
    MyObject* allocate() { /* carve out next free slot */ }
    void deallocate(MyObject* obj) { /* mark slot free */ }
    ~ObjectPool() { std::free(pool); }
};
```

- **Trivial allocator**: Many containers (e.g., `std::pmr::monotonic_buffer_resource`) in C++17/20 provide pool‑style allocation out of the box.

**Trade‑offs**: Pools reduce fragmentation and allocation overhead but add complexity. Use them only if profiling shows real benefit.

---

## 7. Debugging & Instrumentation

Even with smart pointers and RAII, bugs can creep in. Use tools:

| Tool | What it does | Typical usage |
|------|--------------|---------------|
| **Valgrind (memcheck)** | Detects leaks, invalid reads/writes | `valgrind --leak-check=full ./app` |
| **AddressSanitizer (ASan)** | Runtime memory error detector | Compile with `-fsanitize=address` |
| **LeakSanitizer (LSan)** | Leak detection (Linux, macOS) | `-fsanitize=leak` |
| **Dr. Memory** | Windows memory debugger | `drmemory.exe -- ./app` |
| **Static analyzers** (clang‑tidy, cppcheck) | Find potential bugs at compile time | `clang-tidy -checks=* -- ./src` |

**Common patterns to look for**

- Uninitialized memory
- Use‑after‑free
- Buffer overruns
- Double delete / mismatched new/delete
- Memory leaks in `new`‑only code paths (e.g., when an exception is thrown before `delete` is executed)

---

## 8. Best Practices

| Practice | Why it matters | How to do it |
|----------|----------------|--------------|
| **Prefer stack over heap** | Simpler, no risk of leaks | Use automatic variables where lifetime fits |
| **Prefer RAII** | Automatic cleanup, exception safety | Wrap resources in classes with destructors |
| **Prefer smart pointers** | Automatic ownership semantics | Use `unique_ptr` for single ownership, `shared_ptr` when shared |
| **Avoid raw `new`/`delete`** | Leads to errors, manual bookkeeping | Use `std::make_unique`, `std::make_shared` |
| **Use containers** | Built‑in safety, performance | `std::vector`, `std::string`, etc. |
| **Profile before optimizing** | Avoid unnecessary complexity | Use `gprof`, `perf`, or language‑specific profilers |
| **Keep code simple** | Easier to reason, maintain, and audit | Favor clarity over micro‑optimizations |

---

## 9. A Mini‑“Memory‑Management” Checklist

1. **Choose the right storage**  
   - Stack for small, short‑lived objects.  
   - Heap when you need polymorphism, variable size, or longer lifetimes.

2. **Wrap heap objects**  
   - `std::unique_ptr` if a single owner.  
   - `std::shared_ptr` + `std::weak_ptr` if shared.

3. **Use containers**  
   - For collections; they hide allocation details.

4. **If you write a custom class that owns raw memory**  
   - Implement destructor.  
   - Delete copy ctor/assignment; implement move semantics.  
   - Prefer `std::allocator` or `std::pmr` if you need a custom allocator.

5. **Test**  
   - Compile with `-Wall -Wextra -pedantic -g`.  
   - Run with `ASan`, `Valgrind`.  
   - Check code coverage and run-time memory.

6. **Document ownership**  
   - Use comments or `[[nodiscard]]` to signal ownership transfer.  
   - Adopt a naming convention (`p`, `ptr`, `ptr_ref`, etc.) to indicate raw vs owning pointers.

---

## 10. Quick Code Snippet Summary

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <memory>
#include <vector>

// 1. Stack
void stackExample() {
    int x = 5;          // automatically destroyed
}

// 2. Heap with RAII (unique_ptr)
std::unique_ptr<int> heapExample() {
    return std::make_unique<int>(42);
}

// 3. Shared ownership
struct Node {
    std::shared_ptr<Node> next;
};

std::shared_ptr<Node> sharedExample() {
    auto head = std::make_shared<Node>();
    head->next = std::make_shared<Node>();
    // Circular ref? use weak_ptr to break it
    return head;
}

// 4. Container
void containerExample() {
    std::vector<int> v{1,2,3};
    v.reserve(10);   // pre‑allocate
    v.push_back(4);  // no reallocation yet
}

// 5. Custom allocator (simplified)
template<typename T>
struct MyAlloc {
    using value_type = T;
    T* allocate(std::size_t n) { return static_cast<T*>(::operator new(n*sizeof(T))); }
    void deallocate(T* p, std::size_t) noexcept { ::operator delete(p); }
};

void allocatorExample() {
    std::vector<int, MyAlloc<int>> v;  // uses MyAlloc
}

int main() {
    stackExample();
    auto p = heapExample();
    std::cout << *p << '\n';
    sharedExample();
    containerExample();
    allocatorExample();
}
```

---

### Bottom Line

- **C++ memory management is a spectrum** from the automatic stack to manual heap allocation, all the way to high‑level abstractions like containers and smart pointers.
- **Always aim for RAII**: tie resource lifetimes to object lifetimes.  
- **Prefer smart pointers over raw `new`/`delete`**; they give you safety and clarity.
- **Leverage containers and standard allocators** before rolling your own pools.
- **Use tools** to catch bugs early; a memory‑leak or double delete can be catastrophic in production.

With these principles, you’ll write code that is both efficient and reliable, keeping the dreaded “memory bug” at bay. Happy coding!
